WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.562
Why a register first?

2
00:00:01.562 --> 00:00:04.950
Because the later steps can't
be proved complete without it.

3
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.530
By the end of this chapter you can build
the register the guide's Step 2 asks for:

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.541
one row per exposure with its
resource, type, public name,

5
00:00:14.541 --> 00:00:18.900
owner, DDoS state, and its WAF
and mode, regenerated every week.

6
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.693
Every ownerless row is flagged, every
new exposure since last week reported,

7
00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:28.562
every one that went away reported with
a reminder to remove its DNS record, and

8
00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:33.812
every resource Defender for Cloud calls
internet-exposed that the register lacks.

9
00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:35.244
It's for platform owners,

10
00:00:35.244 --> 00:00:39.112
workload owners and the developers
who keep the queries current.

11
00:00:39.112 --> 00:00:43.970
And the register is a map of the attack
surface: it stays inside the boundary.
